/**
 * User consent management: scope permissions, GDPR purposes, and terms-of-use acceptance.
 *
 * <p>This bounded context owns all consent-related obligations that must be satisfied before an
 * authorization session can complete. It handles three distinct consent dimensions — OAuth scope
 * approval per client, GDPR purpose consent for the platform, and terms-of-use acceptance for
 * the relying party — as well as an optional scope-approval workflow where scopes require
 * administrator pre-approval before they can be granted to a requesting client.
 *
 * <p><b>Key domain types:</b>
 * <ul>
 *   <li>{@code ScopePermission} — value object describing a single OAuth scope from the user's
 *       perspective. Carries the scope name, a human-readable label, a localized description,
 *       and a {@code required} flag indicating whether the scope is mandatory (cannot be denied)
 *       or optional (user may decline without aborting the authorization flow).</li>
 *   <li>{@code TermsOfUseAcceptance} — value object recording a user's acceptance of a specific
 *       version of the relying party's terms of use. Carries the accepted version identifier,
 *       the acceptance timestamp, and the client_id to which the terms belong, enabling
 *       version-bump re-prompt logic.</li>
 *   <li>{@code GdprConsentPurposeItem} — value object representing a single GDPR processing
 *       purpose that the user must acknowledge. Carries the purpose identifier, label,
 *       description, legal basis, and current user-consent state (accepted/declined).</li>
 *   <li>{@code ScopesConsentGateway} — outbound port for reading the set of OAuth scopes a user
 *       has previously approved for a client and for persisting newly approved scopes.</li>
 *   <li>{@code TermsOfUseConsentGateway} — port for checking whether a user has accepted the
 *       current version of a client's terms and for recording acceptance with timestamp.</li>
 *   <li>{@code GdprConsentGateway} — port for loading the platform's GDPR purposes for a user
 *       and persisting accept/decline decisions for each purpose.</li>
 *   <li>{@code ScopeApprovalGateway} — port for the administrator pre-approval workflow: check
 *       whether a requested scope requires approval and whether it has already been granted for
 *       a specific client, blocking token issuance until approval is recorded.</li>
 * </ul>
 *
 * <p><b>Key application services:</b>
 * <ul>
 *   <li>{@code ScopesConsentUsecase} — computes the delta between requested scopes and already-
 *       consented scopes for a (user, client) pair, persists the newly approved set, and returns
 *       the pending scope list for the HTML consent step to render.</li>
 *   <li>{@code TermsOfUseConsentUsecase} — checks the currently required terms version against
 *       the user's last accepted version and, when a new version is pending, returns the terms
 *       text for the consent UI step and records acceptance when the user submits.</li>
 *   <li>{@code GdprConsentUsecase} — loads GDPR purposes, records the user's per-purpose
 *       decision, and signals whether all mandatory purposes have been accepted.</li>
 *   <li>{@code ScopeApprovalUsecase} — evaluates whether requested scopes need administrator
 *       pre-approval and whether such approval is already on record, gating the flow accordingly.</li>
 * </ul>
 *
 * <p><b>Infrastructure adapters:</b>
 * <ul>
 *   <li>{@code RequiredConsentService} — infrastructure service that aggregates the three consent
 *       checks (scopes, terms, GDPR) into a single evaluation used by the Authentication context
 *       to determine whether the USE_CONSENT challenge must be presented.</li>
 *   <li>{@code ScopesConsentAdapter} / {@code TermsOfUseConsentAdapter} / {@code GdprConsentAdapter}
 *       — driven SQL adapters for each of the three consent gateway contracts.</li>
 *   <li>{@code NoOpScopeApprovalAdapter} — default no-op driven adapter for the scope-approval
 *       gateway; replaced by a real implementation when the approval workflow is activated.</li>
 * </ul>
 *
 * <p><b>Internal structure:</b>
 * <pre>
 * consent/
 * ├── domain/
 * │   ├── ScopePermission.java / TermsOfUseAcceptance.java / GdprConsentPurposeItem.java
 * │   └── gateway/   — ScopesConsentGateway, TermsOfUseConsentGateway,
 * │                    GdprConsentGateway, ScopeApprovalGateway
 * ├── application/
 * │   └── ScopesConsentUsecase / TermsOfUseConsentUsecase / GdprConsentUsecase / ScopeApprovalUsecase
 * └── infrastructure/
 *     ├── RequiredConsentService.java
 *     └── driven/    — ScopesConsentAdapter, TermsOfUseConsentAdapter,
 *                      GdprConsentAdapter, NoOpScopeApprovalAdapter
 * </pre>
 *
 * <p><b>Dependencies:</b> Invoked by the Authentication context during the USE_CONSENT and
 * CLIENT_CONSENT challenge steps. {@code ScopeApprovalUsecase} may block token issuance until
 * an administrator records approval via an out-of-band management API.
 *
 * <p><b>Stability:</b> moderate; the GDPR and terms-of-use subsystems are policy-driven and
 * may evolve as regulatory requirements change. Core scope-consent logic is stable.
 */
